<section class="gsrp-ep" id="ep-10900"><h2>New Hampshire By The Numbers</h2><div class="gsra-meta">Made October 11, 2026 &middot; <span class="gsra-voice">AI hosts</span> &middot; 13:25</div><p>A tour of more than twenty GSR explainers: the Right-to-Know request builder, Election Day’s holiday status, the school tax cap court orders, the House’s size, pay and consent calendar, the state’s tax mix and amendment rule, and the 1788 ratification. The second half covers ice-out, moose, unions, rent, broadband, poverty, veterans’ care, school enrollment, radon, greenhouse cannabis, press law, wiretaps, the license plate case, small police departments and UFO footage.</p><audio controls preload="none" src="https://granitestatereport.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/posts-to-podcast-1791723628.wav"></audio><h3>Articles discussed in this episode</h3><ul><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/09/29/gsr-tools/">GSR Tools</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/04/election-day-legal-holiday-new-hampshire-mill-factory-law/">Election Day Is a Legal Holiday in New Hampshire. A 1913 Law Makes It a Crime to Require Factory Work on a Legal Holiday.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/05/school-tax-cap-question-court-orders-public-hearings/">The School Tax Cap Question Survived Three Court Orders. The Public Hearings the Judge Mentioned Are Optional.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/05/nh-online-testimony-no-duty-to-answer/">170,000 Online Sign-Ins. No Duty to Answer One.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/11/new-hampshire-house-residents-per-seat-pay-amendments/">New Hampshire Has One State Representative for Every 3,444 Residents. In California, Each Assembly Member Represents 494,228.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/10/win-through-actions-nh-house-consent-calendar/">Win Through Actions, Not Argument</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/11/nh-state-tax-collections-business-income-census-fiscal-2025/">Taxes on Business Income Brought In a Third of New Hampshire’s State Tax Collections in Fiscal 2025. The Next-Highest State Was Alaska, at About a Fifth.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/11/new-hampshire-constitutional-amendments-two-thirds-rule/">Nine Constitutional Amendments Won a Majority of New Hampshire Voters From 1986 Through 2024 and Failed Anyway. The Latest Missed Two-Thirds by About a Point.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/11/new-hampshire-ratified-constitution-1788-57-47-ninth-state/">New Hampshire Ratified the Constitution by 10 Votes. Four Months Earlier, Its Convention Adjourned Rather Than Lose.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/05/lake-winnipesaukee-ice-out-record-1887-usgs/">Lake Winnipesaukee’s Ice-Out Record Goes Back to 1887. The Four Earliest Calls Have All Come Since 2010.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/05/new-hampshire-moose-population-winter-ticks-2026-hunt/">New Hampshire’s Moose Herd Fell From About 7,500 to About 3,000. Here Is What the State’s Own Records Show.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/10/nh-union-membership-rate-lowest-new-england-bls/">New Hampshire Had New England’s Lowest Union Membership Rate Two Years Running. Here Is the 14-Year Federal Record, and Its Limits.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/11/new-hampshire-rent-survey-two-bedroom-county-city-2024/">Lebanon’s Median Two-Bedroom Rent Was $2,923. Coos County’s Was $1,161. Here Is What the State’s Rent Survey Shows, Place by Place.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/11/nh-households-no-internet-subscription-census-bead-broadband/">Census Five-Year Estimates Put 38,414 New Hampshire Households Without an Internet Subscription. The State’s Federal Broadband Build Reaches 5,135 Locations.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/10/nh-poverty-rate-median-household-income-by-county-census-saipe/">New Hampshire Has Had the Lowest Estimated Poverty Rate of Any State in Every Census Estimate Since 2007. In Coos County, the Rate Is Nearly Double the State’s.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/10/va-spending-new-hampshire-fy2025-community-care-veterans/">The VA Spent $1.44 Billion in New Hampshire in Fiscal 2025. Nearly Two of Every Five of Its Medical Dollars Paid for Care Outside the VA.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/11/new-hampshire-public-school-enrollment-decline-2000-2023-nces/">New Hampshire’s Public Schools Lost a Fifth of Their Students From 2000 to 2023. No State Lost a Larger Share.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/11/new-hampshire-radon-test-data-homes-wells/">More Than a Third of New Hampshire Homes Tested for Radon Came Back at or Above the Federal Action Level. Here Is What the State’s Own Data Show.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/10/sb-468-medical-cannabis-greenhouse-law-health-department-rules/">SB 468 Lets New Hampshire’s Medical Cannabis Centers Ask to Grow in Greenhouses. The Health Department’s Rules Decide Where and How.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/05/wikileaks-assange-snowden-what-they-exposed-what-changed/">What WikiLeaks, Julian Assange and Edward Snowden Exposed, and What It Changed</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/10/new-hampshire-wiretaps-federal-wiretap-report-rsa-570-a/">New Hampshire’s State Courts Reported Two Wiretaps in a Decade of Federal Records. Federal Judges in New Hampshire Reported 24.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/05/wooley-v-maynard-live-free-or-die-license-plate-case/">He Taped Over “Live Free or Die” and Went to Jail. His Case Still Shapes Who Can Be Forced to Speak.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/11/nh-police-agency-size-internal-affairs-law-rsa-106-l/">More Than Two of Every Three New Hampshire Police Agencies Reported 10 or Fewer Full-Time Officers. State Law Requires Each One to Run an Internal Affairs Program.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/11/send-ufo-paranormal-wildlife-footage/">Granite State Report Takes UFO, Paranormal and Wildlife Footage. Here Is How Every Clip Gets Checked.</a></li></ul><div class="gsrp-note"><p><strong>Listener&rsquo;s note.</strong></p><ul><li>The hosts open with “Dexter Dow has been busy.” Two of the 24 articles carry his byline: the online testimony piece and “Win Through Actions, Not Argument.” The GSR Tools page is credited to Granite State Report, and the other 21 are bylined GSR Staff.</li><li>The hosts quote RSA 91-A:4 as saying “Every citizen may inspect all governmental records in a public body’s possession, custody, or control, and copy them, except as prohibited by statute or RSA 91-A:5.” That sentence is not in the statute or in any Granite State Report article. The GSR Tools post says only that the builder writes a request that cites RSA 91-A:4. The statute, RSA 91-A:4, I, reads: “Every citizen during the regular or business hours of all public bodies or agencies, and on the regular business premises of such public bodies or agencies, has the right to inspect all governmental records in the possession, custody, or control of such public bodies or agencies, including minutes of meetings of the public bodies, and to copy and make memoranda or abstracts of the records or minutes so inspected, except as otherwise prohibited by statute or RSA 91-A:5.”</li><li>The hosts say the GSR Tools post “flags that under paragraph IX, the fee waiver for public-interest disclosure expressly excludes media requestors.” The GSR Tools post says nothing about paragraph IX, fees or media requesters. Paragraph IX governs waivers of the per-communication charge in paragraph VIII; it does not change who may see records, and every person has the same right of access under RSA 91-A. Granite State Report’s reporting treats the media clause as unsettled: “Seven Laws Changed New Hampshire’s Right-to-Know Statute Since 2024” says the statute does not settle what the carve-out does.</li><li>The hosts say “Eight bills to give that holiday real meaning have died since 2014.” The article says “Between 2014 and 2025 at least eight bills proposed to close public offices on election days, add primaries to the list, or give workers time to vote. None became law.” The hosts drop “at least.”</li><li>The hosts say the 1788 ratification “cleared its own supermajority by exactly ten votes.” The article describes no supermajority. The convention voted 57 to 47, and “The convention’s journal records 57 yeas and 47 nays, a majority of 10.” The threshold the article names is Article VII’s nine states: the vote “met the threshold the Constitution set for itself.” A simple majority of the convention decided it.</li><li>The hosts say New Hampshire’s corporation net income share was “the highest share of any state for the tenth consecutive year.” The article says New Hampshire “has held the top spot on that measure in every one of the bureau’s annual counts from fiscal 2016 through fiscal 2025, the 10 years in this analysis.” The article did not look before fiscal 2016, so it does not say the run began there.</li><li>The hosts say of the remote-participation rule in RSA 91-A:2, IV that “The Legislature and every town are explicitly excluded from that requirement.” The article says paragraph IV “exempts the General Court, both chambers and every committee of either house.” Towns are not named in any exclusion. They fall under paragraph III, and the article says Granite State Report “found nothing in the section that requires a town, city or school board to let the public join remotely.”</li><li>The hosts say the bill that would have required committees to explain what they did with online testimony “died on a 123-207 vote.” The article says the House “laid it on the table by voice vote on March 5, 2026, refused to take it back up 123-207 a week later, and let it die when the session ended.” The 123-207 vote was on taking the bill off the table, not on the bill.</li><li>The hosts say “the state’s own announcements have used figures ranging from 3,000 to 4,000 depending on which release you read.” The article says the announcements said the population “is estimated to be about 3,000 animals” or that New Hampshire “has about 3,300 moose.” The 3,000 to 4,000 range comes from the department’s undated moose profile page: “about 3,000-4,000.”</li><li>The hosts say community care’s 38.2 percent share was “fourth-highest nationally, partly because the state has no full-service VA hospital.” The article ranks it fourth “Among the 50 states” (Guam was higher, at 58.1 percent) and says “The report itself does not explain any state’s share, and the overlap is Granite State Report’s observation, not a finding by the VA.” The hosts turn an observed overlap into a cause.</li><li>The hosts describe the Mortgage Specialists case as involving “a redacted loan chart.” The article says the website “linked to a document that purported to show the company’s 2007 loan figures” and quotes the court on the “Loan Chart.” It never says the chart was redacted.</li><li>The hosts say Wooley v. Maynard “has been cited in 1,262 opinions since.” The article says “As of October 5, 2026, CourtListener, a free case-law database, listed 1,262 opinions citing Wooley v. Maynard.” The count is one database’s, on one date.</li><li>The hosts refer to “New Hampshire’s 200 law enforcement agencies.” The article says “For 2024, 200 New Hampshire agencies answered with a number” and that the FBI’s explorer “listed 211 New Hampshire agencies.” 200 is the count of agencies with a 2024 figure, not the total.</li></ul><p>Checked and holding: the GSR Tools post offers a free, in-browser Right-to-Know request builder that cites RSA 91-A:4 and needs no form; it computes the five-business-day deadline skipping weekends and RSA 288:1 holidays; the Oct. 5, 2026 correction made the builder apply RSA 288:2 to holidays falling on a Sunday; Election Day is on the RSA 288:1 list and has been a legal holiday since 1891; state offices stay open; the 1913 mill law makes requiring mill or factory work on a legal holiday a misdemeanor for a person and a felony for a company; the 2025 bill came out of committee 9-7 to pass and lost on the floor 166-205; the school tax cap question got past a superior court judge, one Supreme Court justice and four justices in 12 days; Judge St. Hilaire’s quotation on publications, radio addresses and public hearing commentary; RSA 32:5-i allows a hearing and requires none; neither Supreme Court order states reasons; more than 170,000 online sign-ins in 2025; Citizens Count’s about one-in-three match at 100 or more sign-ins; the Harvard authors’ quotation that no U.S. government has conferred decision-making power in an electronic venue; 400 seats, 1,377,529 residents, 3,444 per seat, the smallest ratio of any state chamber; $200 per two-year term in the constitution; four proposals from 2019 through 2026 to change size or pay died in the House; consent calendar placement takes a unanimous committee vote, ten members can remove a bill, and a removed bill goes to the end of the Regular Calendar; $3.37 billion in fiscal 2025 state tax collections, $1.11 billion or 32.9 percent on corporation net income, Alaska next at 21.7 percent; the individual income line fell by half the year the Interest and Dividends Tax repeal took effect; amendments need two-thirds of those voting on the question; nine won a majority and failed from 1986 through 2024; the 2024 retirement age amendment drew 65.6 percent and fell about 7,400 votes short; 57 to 47 in Concord on June 21, 1788, the ninth ratification under Article VII; the February convention adjourned rather than lose; a six-delegate switch would have reversed it; the USGS table covers 122 springs, 1887 through 2008, with two March ice-outs; at least four March ice-outs since 2010; earliest on record March 17, 2024; the moose herd peaked at 7,000 to 8,000 in the late 1990s and is about 3,000 now; 88 percent of radio-marked calf deaths tied to moderate or severe winter tick infestations; union membership 9.2 percent in 2024 and 8.9 percent in 2025, lowest in New England both years, with the bureau’s caution on 2025 state figures after the October shutdown gap; statewide median two-bedroom $1,833 in 2024, up 58 percent from 2015; Lebanon $2,923 and Coos $1,161; in every county the median two-bedroom exceeded what the county’s median renter income could afford at 30 percent; 13.4 percent of Coos households and 4.9 percent in Rockingham without an internet subscription; Coos poverty 13.8 percent, nearly double the state’s 7.3 percent, the lowest estimated state rate for 18 straight years; VA spending of $1.44 billion in fiscal 2025 with community care at 38.2 percent of medical spending; public school enrollment down 20.1 percent from 2000 to 2023, the largest drop of any state; more than a third of homes tested for radon at or above 4.0 pCi/L; SB 468 became law on the Aug. 19, 2026 veto override and lets alternative treatment centers ask to grow in greenhouses, under department rules with no deadline; Mortgage Specialists v. Implode-Explode (2010), its quotation on lawfully obtained information, and “no less a member of the press”; the Assange case turned on how he obtained the material; two state-court wiretaps in ten federal reports, both for 2019 and both drug cases, and 24 federal orders from the District of New Hampshire; George Maynard served 15 days in jail in 1975 rather than pay fines, and the Supreme Court ruled for him in 1977; more than two-thirds of reporting agencies had 10 or fewer full-time officers in 2024, median six, and since 2023 every agency must keep an internal affairs program and report certain cases to a state review committee; the Air Force’s 1965 Exeter file and the newsroom’s use of it as the standard for every clip; NHMA counsel’s “entirely the choice of the municipality”; state boards meeting remotely must let the public take part remotely under RSA 91-A:2, IV.</p></div><details class="gsrp-tx"><summary>Read the transcript</summary><p><strong>Pip:</strong> Granite State Report covers the state where your license plate is legally required to tell you how to feel about death, and your government is legally required to ignore your opinion about it. Dexter Dow has been busy.</p><p><strong>Mara:</strong> This episode covers the full range of what that means in practice: tools for filing records requests, the school tax cap&#039;s journey through three court orders, how New Hampshire&#039;s legislature is structured and funded, what the state&#039;s tax mix looks like, and how its constitution gets changed.</p><p><strong>Pip:</strong> Plus moose, radon, rent, veterans, Assange, a 1975 Jehovah&#039;s Witness, and 170,000 sign-ins that nobody had to answer. Let&#039;s start with state finances and governance.</p></details></section>
<section class="gsrp-ep" id="ep-10472"><h2>Hire, Run, Fire: The Judge, the Clerk and the Birthday</h2><div class="gsra-meta">Made October 5, 2026 &middot; <span class="gsra-voice">Scripted from the articles</span> &middot; 19:21</div><p>Three entries from the Jobs in the Law series in one conversation: the circuit judges who carry nine of every ten cases, the appointed clerks who decide what a case is made of, and the two removal tools New Hampshire has never finished using, set beside the age-70 rule that has never failed. Hosts June and Theo are synthetic voices.</p><audio controls preload="none" src="https://granitestatereport.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GSR_Podcast_HireRunFire_v1_2026-10-05.mp3"></audio><h3>Articles discussed in this episode</h3><ul><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/08/20/what-a-new-hampshire-judge-does-all-day/">Inside the Job: What a New Hampshire Judge Does All Day</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/08/24/clerk-of-court-new-hampshire-job/">The Clerk of the Court in New Hampshire: Real Power, No Ballot Line</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/08/21/how-to-remove-a-new-hampshire-judge-and-why-it-almost-never-happens/">How to Remove a New Hampshire Judge &mdash; and Why It Almost Never Happens</a></li></ul><div class="gsrp-note"><p><strong>How this episode was made.</strong> The script was written by Claude, an AI model made by Anthropic, from the three articles linked above, and every factual line in it traces to a sentence in one of them. Where the hosts talk about how an article is built, that is their reading of the article, not new reporting. The two voices are synthetic, generated with Kokoro, an open-weight text-to-speech model. None of the articles is legal advice, and neither is this episode.</p></div><details class="gsrp-tx"><summary>Read the transcript</summary><p><strong>June:</strong> This is the GSR Podcast, from Granite State Report in Northfield, New Hampshire. I&rsquo;m June.</p><p><strong>Theo:</strong> And I&rsquo;m Theo. Today, three entries from the Jobs in the Law series, and they work best in one sitting. What a judge does all day. Who runs the counter in front of the judge. And how you get a judge off the bench.</p><p><strong>June:</strong> Hire, run, fire. The whole life cycle of the robe.</p></details></section>
<section class="gsrp-ep" id="ep-10527"><h2>New Hampshire Democracy And Records</h2><div class="gsra-meta">Made October 5, 2026 &middot; <span class="gsra-voice">AI hosts</span> &middot; 7:41</div><p>Three subjects in one episode: what the WikiLeaks and Snowden disclosures exposed and what they changed, the index New Hampshire never built and the archive made to fill the gap, and two proposed constitutions offered for argument ahead of the 2032 convention question.</p><p class="gsra-note"><em>Generated by the WordPress.com podcast tool on October 5, 2026. On October 10 the script was revised and the episode re-recorded with synthetic voices. The player and the transcript carry the revised version.</em></p><audio controls preload="none" src="https://granitestatereport.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/gsr-podcast-10527-neural.mp3"></audio><h3>Articles discussed in this episode</h3><ul><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/10/05/wikileaks-assange-snowden-what-they-exposed-what-changed/">What WikiLeaks, Julian Assange and Edward Snowden Exposed, and What It Changed</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/09/17/new-hampshire-record-index/">New Hampshire Will Sell You a Record. It Will Never Build You an Index.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/08/26/aclu-lawsuit-nh-state-police-287g-right-to-know-delay/">Fourteen Months to Answer a Five-Day Law</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/09/16/attorney-general-right-to-know-manual-rsa-91-a-graded/">The Attorney General’s Right-to-Know Manual Leaves Out the Part Where You Get the Records</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/09/16/what-changed-in-new-hampshires-right-to-know-law/">Seven Laws Changed New Hampshire’s Right-to-Know Statute Since 2024. Here Is Every Word That Moved.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/07/29/nh-right-to-know-ombudsman-vacant-13-months/">New Hampshire’s Right-to-Know Ombudsman Seat Has Been Empty 13 Months. The Law Still Sends People There.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/09/17/record-room/">Granite State Record Room</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/09/16/one-word-in-new-hampshires-private-investigator-law-decides-who-counts-as-press-nobody-has-defined-it/">One Word in New Hampshire’s Private Investigator Law Decides Who Counts as Press. Nobody Has Defined It.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/09/15/a-new-constitution-for-new-hampshire-and-the-united-states/">A New Constitution for New Hampshire. Another for the United States. Here Is How It Can Legally Be Done.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/09/15/new-hampshire-constitution-each-generation/">New Hampshire Asks Every Ten Years Whether to Rewrite Its Constitution. The Country Never Has To.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/09/04/live-free-or-die-is-a-slogan-here-is-the-ledger/">Live Free or Die Is a Slogan. Here Is the Ledger.</a></li></ul><div class="gsrp-note"><p><strong>Listener&rsquo;s note.</strong></p><ul><li>The hosts say the 2010 ruling meant New Hampshire “quietly settled the ‘is a website press’ question.” The article fences the ruling in: “The privilege it recognized is qualified. The dispute was commercial, with no national-security claim.” It adds that the ruling “binds New Hampshire courts, not federal courts or federal prosecutors.” Settled is stronger than the article goes.</li><li>The hosts say Snowden, as of the newsroom’s October 2026 reporting, “remained in Russia.” The article is more careful: “This newsroom found no report that he has left Russia, that the 2013 charges have been dropped, or that he has been pardoned.” Its fact-check row adds: “A search that finds nothing does not prove nothing exists.”</li><li>The WikiLeaks segment gives only the defenders’ record. The article says it “sets out the public record behind the case that defenders of WikiLeaks, Julian Assange and Edward Snowden make, and the record their critics cite against it,” and gives a full section to the critics: the unredacted cables, the Mueller report’s finding that the DNC emails were “stolen by the GRU,” and the House Intelligence Committee report.</li><li>The hosts say the ACLU of New Hampshire “waited fourteen months for State Police records.” Fourteen months is the State Police’s own projected timeline, not time already waited: “The New Hampshire State Police told the American Civil Liberties Union it would need until November 24, 2026 to answer a public records request the ACLU filed on September 12, 2025.” The ACLU sued on August 25, 2026, about eleven and a half months after filing.</li><li>The hosts say “seven amendments to the statute have passed since it was issued without a rewrite.” The index article says “Seven separate acts have amended RSA 91-A since that memorandum was issued, and the memorandum has not been rewritten.” Seven acts, not seven amendments, and as spoken the line attaches “without a rewrite” to the statute, when the unrewritten thing is the memorandum.</li><li>The hosts say “Four hundred dollars a day, no takers.” The figure is real: RSA 91-A:7-a pays “four hundred dollars for each day devoted to the work of the office” (index article and ombudsman article). “No takers” also has a source: the governor’s “staff told the Monitor in March that no one had applied” (ombudsman article). But the line turns the articles’ point upside down. Said alone, $400 a day sounds generous. The ombudsman article says “Reporting puts the appropriation behind those rates at $30,000, down from a full-time salaried office,” works out about 62 days a year at that rate, and the index article says the state “funded it like a stipend for a part-time position on a zoning board.”</li><li>The hosts say the Ledger column counts “a right-to-know office defunded and vacant.” Vacant holds. Defunded does not: “Then the Legislature cut the office’s pay and budget from $105,000 to $30,000.”</li><li>The hosts present the Ledger column as running “the numbers on what that frozen machinery produces,” tying it to the constitutional-amendment argument. The column places the causes in statute and in Concord, not in the constitution: “A flaw in the design cannot be voted out. A capture can.” It ends: “The minimum wage is set by the Legislature. The ombudsman’s budget was cut by the Legislature.” The “machinery has frozen” framing comes from the New Constitution essay, not the Ledger.</li><li>The hosts say RSA 106-F “carves out ‘accredited’ news organizations” and that this statute is “the reason” the records are free while the labor is paid. The exclusion covers people, not organizations: “persons engaged in reporting or providing information to an accredited news publication, media, or news outlet.” The index article stresses that the wording matters: “Whether it describes a sole proprietor who is the outlet is a question the text does not answer.” It also calls RSA 106-F “a second reason for that line,” not the only one.</li></ul><p>Checked and holding: Mortgage Specialists v. Implode-Explode, 2010, Justice Conboy for a unanimous court, quotation verbatim; the ruling came one month after the April 5, 2010 Baghdad gun-camera video and three years before Snowden’s first disclosure; two federal appeals courts held the phone-records program unlawful and Congress ended it; an estimated 15,000 civilian deaths missing from Iraq Body Count’s tally; Assange’s 2024 guilty plea to one Espionage Act count; Snowden charged since 2013; the source-and-publisher closing line; RSA 91-A:4, VII quoted verbatim; the library-with-no-catalog image; the AG memorandum leaving out the part where requesters get the records; the $25 ombudsman route as the alternative to superior court; the seat vacant since July 1, 2025, when the first ombudsman’s term ended; every pending case dismissed; the seat still unfilled as of the July 2026 reporting; $400 a day is the statutory rate; 29 items across four series at publication, six of them RSA 91-A records; provenance stated for each item; the published exclusions list and the brochure line; RSA 106-F leaves accredited undefined and no state agency issues the credential; commissions limited to public-interest work, everything obtained published free to everyone, editorial control not transferred; New Hampshire adopted America’s first constitution; the convention question every ten years and on the 2032 ballot; Jefferson to Madison, 1789, mortality tables, “The earth belongs always to the living generation” verbatim; 935 national constitutions with an average lifespan of nineteen years; Madison’s 1790 reply won the argument; the federal constitution not amended in the living memory of most Americans now alive; Article 100’s fixed schedule against Article V’s lack of any clock; minimum wage at $7.25 since 2009; wealth concentration; both drafts labeled works of argument, not law; the New Hampshire draft keeps the 400-member House, the Right of Revolution word for word and the decennial question, and writes the education duty so a statute cannot recast it as a shared responsibility; the federal draft elects the president by national popular vote, lifts the 1929 House cap, and requires a convention every nineteen years with the existing constitution in force between drafts; delegates elected from House districts, about four hundred; ratification needs two-thirds of the voters; the byline split, with the press-freedom piece credited to the newsroom (GSR Staff) and the records and constitution pieces to Dexter Dow.</p></div><details class="gsrp-tx"><summary>Read the transcript</summary><p><em>Two synthetic voices read this episode. It summarizes Granite State Report articles; each claim was checked against those articles and their sources.</em></p><p><strong>Pip:</strong> Granite State Report covers the state that wrote America&#039;s first constitution, still hasn&#039;t defined what &quot;accredited&quot; means for a news outlet, and asks its voters every ten years whether the rulebook needs updating. Welcome to the episode.</p><p><strong>Mara:</strong> The newsroom has been doing the work here — press freedom and what Snowden and Assange changed, and, under Dexter Dow&#039;s byline, New Hampshire&#039;s public records gap, the archive built to fill it, and a pair of proposed constitutions aimed at the 2032 ballot and beyond. Let&#039;s start with the question of what leaks cost and what they bought.</p></details></section>
<section class="gsrp-ep" id="ep-6386"><h2>New Hampshire Records and Benefits</h2><div class="gsra-meta">Made September 17, 2026 &middot; <span class="gsra-voice">AI hosts</span> &middot; 6:00</div><p>Three pieces in one episode: how RSA 91-A has moved since 2024, the index New Hampshire never built and the Record Room built to replace it, and the SNAP work notice that turns an “or” into a requirement.</p><p class="gsra-note"><em>Generated by the WordPress.com podcast tool on September 17, 2026. On October 10 the script was revised and the episode re-recorded with synthetic voices. The player and the transcript carry the revised version.</em></p><audio controls preload="none" src="https://granitestatereport.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/gsr-podcast-6386-neural.mp3"></audio><h3>Articles discussed in this episode</h3><ul><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/09/16/what-changed-in-new-hampshires-right-to-know-law/">Seven Laws Changed New Hampshire’s Right-to-Know Statute Since 2024. Here Is Every Word That Moved.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/09/16/rsa-91-a-redline/">RSA 91-A Redline: Every Change Since the Attorney General’s March 2024 Memorandum</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/09/17/new-hampshire-record-index/">New Hampshire Will Sell You a Record. It Will Never Build You an Index.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/archive/">Granite State Archive (formerly the Granite State Record Room)</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/09/16/one-word-in-new-hampshires-private-investigator-law-decides-who-counts-as-press-nobody-has-defined-it/">One Word in New Hampshire’s Private Investigator Law Decides Who Counts as Press. Nobody Has Defined It.</a></li><li><a href="https://granitestatereport.com/2026/09/16/new-hampshire-snap-work-notice/">New Hampshire’s SNAP Work Notice Misstates Rules It Was Written to Explain</a></li></ul><details class="gsrp-tx"><summary>Read the transcript</summary><p><em>Two synthetic voices read this episode. It summarizes Granite State Report articles; each claim was checked against those articles and their sources.</em></p><p><strong>Pip:</strong> Granite State Report covers New Hampshire, where the right to know what your government is doing is technically guaranteed, and technically is doing a lot of work in that sentence.</p><p><strong>Mara:</strong> Dexter Dow has been tracking that gap closely. We&#039;re looking at how New Hampshire&#039;s public-records law has shifted since 2024, what tools exist to work with it, and where a state SNAP notice goes wrong in ways that cost real people real protections.</p></details></section>
